  "subject_executed": "Preprocessing mismatch diagnosis for Blood_5 monocyte collapse. Compared exact PBC vs Blood_5 preprocessing pipelines (resize, interpolation, channel order, dtype, value range, normalization). Measured per-channel pixel statistics for 200-image samples from both datasets after preprocessing. Re-evaluated Blood_5 under matched pipeline and BGR->RGB corrected pipeline. Control: PBC test set through Blood_5 numpy-roundtrip path. Sanity-checked 5 Blood_5 sample images.",
  "notes": "VERDICT: genuine_domain_shift. Both datasets use identical VAL_TF (Resize 224x224 BILINEAR, ToTensor [0,1], Normalize ImageNet [0.485,0.456,0.406]/[0.229,0.224,0.225]). BGR->RGB swap test rules out channel-order mismatch (swap makes accuracy 12.5%->1.9%, worse not better). PBC numpy-roundtrip control gives identical accuracy (delta=0.000), ruling out loading-path artifacts. Blood_5 images are valid single-cell crops (uint8, full range, plausible bright/dark pixel ratios). The monocyte collapse is driven by genuine distributional shift: Blood_5 has systematically higher blue channel (B>R>G, raw means ~205/180/163) vs PBC (R>G>B, raw means ~218/184/183). After ImageNet normalization, Blood_5 B-channel mean=+1.512 vs PBC +1.387, while Blood_5 R-channel mean=+0.814 vs PBC +1.633 -- a large and genuine distributional difference. Data stored in HWC format (confirmed by corner-pixel analysis: white-background corners R=226,G=239,B=230 in HWC vs G=138 in CHW interpretation). Dataset provenance: CIFAR-style pickle in Zenodo record doi:10.5281/zenodo.21628834, file code_WITH_dataset.zip, path data_local/blood_5/test_batch."
